":{"entries":[["nur bahnhof verstehen",null,"/nuːɐ̯ ˈbaːnhoːf fɐˈʃteːən/","Earlier 20th century. Literally, “to understand only train station”.\nAccording to some sources (e.g. Duden) from WWI military slang. For front soldiers, Bahnhof (“train station”) would have been synonymous with furlough or relocation to the hinterland, so the phrase would originally have meant “to understand only what one wants to hear”, “not to pay attention”. What may corroborate this theory is the variant immer Bahnhof verstehen (literally “to understand train station all the time”).\nOther suggested origins:\n* Train stations are noisy, hectic places with whirring engines, shouting passengers, unintelligible announcements etc.\n* The word Bahnhof (and its equivalents in other languages) would typically have been familiar to international travellers even if they knew hardly any other word of the local language."]],"senses":[[62106,"nur bahnhof verstehen","verb","to understand nothing at all",0,null,["idiomatic"]],[62107,"nur bahnhof verstehen","verb","to deliberately not pay attention to a conversation",1,null,["idiomatic"]]]},"obskurant":{"entries":[["obskurant",null,null,"Borrowed from Latin obscūrāns.",{"g":"m","decl":"|en|en|en|en|en|en|en"}]],"senses":[[676780,"obskurant","noun","obscurant (person who seeks to prevent or hinder enquiry and the advancement of knowledge or wisdom)",0,"m"]]},"plastiktrinkbecher":{"entries":[["plastiktrinkbecher",null,null,"From Plastik (“plastic”) + Trinkbecher (“drinking-cup”).",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s|||n|"}]],"senses":[[621454,"plastiktrinkbecher","noun","plastic drinking-cup",0,"m"]]},"popeligeres":{"entries":[["popeligeres",null,null,null]],"senses":[[533074,"popeligeres","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative neuter singular comparative degree of popelig",0]],"inflections":[["popeligeres","popelig","comparative,neuter,nominative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"rechenverfahrens":{"entries":[["rechenverfahrens",null,"/ˈʁɛçn̩fɛɐ̯ˌfaːʁəns/",null